# B4w — A CROSSING: A RUN BEGINS AT AN END OR A JUNCTION
#
# ⛔ **`B4v` REFUSED TWO WALLS THAT CROSS, AND ITS OWN RECORD NAMED WHY**: the trial had no
# candidate generator for a run. `disc_within` searches centres and shells; a run has no
# such grid — its endpoints are continuous world points.
#
# ✅ **BUT A WALL CAN ONLY BEGIN WHERE THE CHAIN DOES.** A free END is a vertex where one
# mark meets; a JUNCTION is where three or more do. Both are exact integers over the corner
# identity `wall_chain_ends` counts degrees with, so the candidate set is a handful of
# corners — and it is generate-and-compare from there, with `B4v`'s union test unchanged.
#
# ⛔ **A `T` IS A DIFFERENT ANSWER AND IT IS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED.** Its stem ends at a
# continuous point on the crossbar's centreline; of eight stem positions along one
# crossbar, **two** close and six are refused with every mark counted. That is
# `FORMAL_CORE` §6's R1/R2 boundary showing itself, not a gap in the pool.
#
#   make plan-view WORLD=b4w Q0=-16 Q1=16 R0=-14 R1=14 REF=2.0

echo --- two walls that CROSS, which `B4v` refused with all 28 marks counted
at -10 0 0
verb run
at 10 0 0
verb run
at 0 -8 0
verb run
at 0 8 0
verb run
